# Environment
#
# `.env` holds GROQ_API_KEY. Note that this bare pattern is what does the work —
# a pattern like `.env.*` would NOT match a file named exactly `.env`, because
# the `.` there is a literal character with nothing after it to match. That
# near-miss is a common way a real secrets file ends up committed while the
# author believes it is ignored. Verify with `git add --dry-run .`, which lists
# what would actually be staged.
.env
.env.local
venv/
.venv/

# Python
#
# These rules were already here, yet `__pycache__/fs_tools.cpython-37.pyc` was
# tracked anyway — because .gitignore only affects files git is NOT already
# tracking. Adding a rule does nothing to a file that was committed before the
# rule existed; it has to be removed from the index explicitly with
# `git rm --cached`. Symptom that gave it away: `git status` reported the .pyc as
# modified every time the code ran, so simply using the project dirtied the repo.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# Test tooling
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# ChromaDB
chroma_db/

# Vector store
vector_store.json

# Output files
match_results.json
match_results.csv
retrieval_metrics.csv
latency_metrics.csv
*.png

# Anything the assistant writes for itself. write_file is restricted to paths
# inside the project, so its output lands here — generated artifacts, not source.
output/
reports/

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
desktop.ini